  "verbalization": "Ceftriaxone, a cephalosporin antibiotic, is consistently linked to Slc15a2 through a shared hierarchical relationship with other cephalosporins. The evidence suggests that Ceftriaxone, as part of the broader class of cephalosporins, binds to Slc15a2 through a common pathway. This is supported by multiple paths that show Ceftriaxone being included in the cephalosporin group, which in turn binds to Slc15a2. The lowest common ancestors across these paths reinforce the notion that the interaction between Ceftriaxone and Slc15a2 is rooted in the structural and functional properties of cephalosporins. The consistent presence of this relationship across different cephalosporin subclasses, such as cefotaxime, cefuroxime, and cefepime, suggests a general mechanism of action involving Slc15a2. This integrated evidence highlights a recurring mechanism where cephalosporins, through their shared classification, interact with Slc15a2, underscoring the importance of the Slc15a2 gene in the pharmacological profile of these antibiotics.",
